# Working copies stay LF on every OS, including Windows with core.autocrlf=true.
# Prettier --check uses endOfLine=lf (Prettier default); CRLF working trees fail.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The vendored contract is a byte-for-byte copy of the gateway repo's file, and
# test/contract.test.ts pins its sha256. With core.autocrlf on — the Windows
# default, and how this repo is checked out — git would rewrite its LFs to CRLFs
# on checkout, changing that digest and failing the suite on a clean clone while
# passing on the machine that wrote the file. `-text` turns the conversion off
# for this path, so the working copy is what was committed.
contracts/** -text
